\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces The M5StickC Plus2 wearable device. The ESP32-PICO-V3-02 system-on-chip, the six-axis IMU (MPU6886), and the microphone (SPM1423) are all integrated on the board. Image: M5Stack~\cite {m5stickcplus2}.}}{13}{figure.3.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Architecture of the multimodal data-acquisition system. Each of the four wearable devices samples an IMU and a microphone independently, timestamps and frames the samples, and streams them as two separate UDP streams (inertial on one port, audio on another) to a single host, which separates the devices by packet source address and writes raw per-device files.}}{16}{figure.3.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Signal-conditioning and windowing pipeline, applied per device. The inertial and acoustic branches are brought to fixed rates before both are cut on the same window boundaries.}}{20}{figure.3.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Window generation and majority-vote labelling. Two-second windows advance in one-second steps ($W_1$, $W_2$, $W_3$ shown stacked for clarity). A window takes the majority activity among its labelled samples; a window whose labelled coverage falls below 70\,\%, such as one spanning the transition region, is discarded.}}{23}{figure.3.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.5}{\ignorespaces Number of two-second windows per activity class in the dominant-wrist dataset ($n = 1380$). The distribution is dominated by the screwdriving classes; \texttt {screw\_tightening} alone accounts for about 38\,\% of the windows and the two screwdriving classes together for more than half.}}{25}{figure.3.5}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Conversion of a two-second audio window into the log-mel spectrogram used as input to the acoustic models.}}{29}{figure.4.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Audio model with a shared convolutional stem and the three pooling variants: (a) global average pooling, (b) recurrent pooling with a bidirectional GRU, and (c) hybrid pooling, which concatenates both summaries.}}{31}{figure.4.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces The three fusion strategies. Early fusion combines the raw inputs before learning, embedding-level fusion combines learned 64-dimensional representations of two frozen backbones, and decision-level fusion combines the class probabilities of two independently trained models.}}{33}{figure.4.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Decision-level fusion with per-class weights (top) and the coordinate-ascent search used to determine the weights on the validation data (bottom).}}{35}{figure.4.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces Grouped splitting. Overlapping windows are grouped into activity instances, and whole instances are assigned to either the training or the validation set. A random split would distribute the overlapping windows of one instance across both sets.}}{39}{figure.4.5}%
